10. SECURITY REQUIREMENTS

Security is a mandatory part of the implementation.

GitHub Pages is static hosting. A client-side admin page is not inherently private merely because it uses a hidden URL.

Implement the following:

1. Never hard-code secrets.
2. Never expose a GitHub token through public source files.
3. Use HTTPS.
4. Restrict requests to the intended GitHub API origin.
5. Validate repository owner, repository name, branch, paths, and external URLs.
6. Escape user-controlled text before inserting it into the DOM.
7. Avoid unsafe HTML injection.
8. Do not evaluate edited code inside the admin application.
9. Use sandboxed previews for untrusted content.
10. Detect file update conflicts.
11. Protect critical files from accidental deletion.
12. Use explicit confirmation for destructive actions.
13. Implement logout and session cleanup.
14. Avoid unnecessary third-party dependencies.
15. Do not log authentication tokens.
16. Do not store credentials in public repository files.
17. Explain that anyone with a valid token and sufficient permissions can perform the operations allowed by that token.
18. Recommend repository backups and minimal token permissions.

A browser-only implementation cannot guarantee secure authorization of the "/wp-admin/" page itself. If private access to the admin application, secure persistent sessions, or centralized user authorization is required, propose a separate authentication/backend architecture.

Do not claim that obscurity, a hidden URL, or client-side JavaScript provides strong access control.

